(This readme is even more out of date than the code; I'll be getting to it.)


uts 0.14 -- 17 October 2001
by Darius Bacon <darius@accesscom.com>
http://www.accesscom.com/~darius

See the file COPYING for copyright info.

This is a very preliminary release of a Scheme bytecode interpreter.
It works, and mostly conforms to the R4RS standard, but has few frills
and poor documentation.  It's meant to be -- eventually -- 

 - standard-conforming (probably following Scheme48 where it's necessary
   to go beyond R4RS)

 - small and quick to start up

 - C-friendly

 - well documented and easy to extend or embed.


INSTALLATION

This currently needs the Boehm garbage collector:

http://www.hpl.hp.com/personal/Hans_Boehm/gc/
# http://reality.sgi.com/employees/boehm_mti/gc_source/gc.tar.gz

The makefile and sourcecode assume Boehm's gc.h and gc.a are in a
subdirectory `gc' of this directory.

The C code assumes two gcc extensions -- inline procedures and 64-bit
long long ints.  It also assumes ints and longs are 32 bits.  See
config.h.  A port to a different wordsize is likely to take more work
than just fiddling with config.h, though.

Finally, you need awk to generate some of the code.  There's no good
reason for that at the moment, but I had an idea or two for the future
that may make it handy.

The makefile by default compiles a debugging version of the code with
lots of assertions.  `makefast' compiles without the checks.  In fast
mode, the system trusts the compiler to feed it legal bytecode
programs.  (The default mode is much much slower than fast mode.)

You can run the compiled code with 

	./uts uts.fasl

To use Aubrey Jaffer's slib, load slib.init after first setting the OS,
library-vicinity, and implementation-vicinity variables in that file.

To run a particular Scheme program from the shell, 

	./uts uts.fasl -f foo.scm arg1 ... argn

and the args are in a list @command-line-args.

To rebuild uts.fasl from sources,

	$ ./uts uts.fasl 
	-> (load "write-fasl.scm")
	-> (build-system "uts.scm" "uts.fasl")
	-> 

This only works if your OS doesn't distinguish text and binary files
(as in Unix).  However, the C code loads the startup fasl as a binary
file, so you can at least use the prebuilt uts.fasl even on
braindamaged systems.

Always typing ``./uts uts.fasl'' is kind of a pain, so I recommend
changing the interpreter path at the top of uts.fasl to the location
on your system of uts, then placing uts.fasl in your path somewhere
under a name like `scheme'.


KNOWN BUGS

Ack!
-> #x80000000
80000000.

Another one:
-> #\\37777777703
#\\
-> 37777777703.

Also, #\space32 should be scanned as two tokens -- #\space and 32.
(Ugh -- I think I will retain this incompatibility unless I release
this as something with pretensions to usefulness.)

-> (case 42 ((42)))
#t
[is that a bug?]

Most importantly, the global environment is full of implementation
definitions like @EVAL and PARSE-FORM.  If your code redefines these,
things are going to break.  What's worse, if you redefine a standard
procedure like MAP, that can break the compiler too.  I started
wrapping all the system code together, closing over the top-level
variables needed and exporting only a few public definitions, but that
was just too painful to maintain.  The top priority now is a real
module system.

Inexact numbers may not be read and written to full precision, since I
rely on the standard C library.

You need to (set! @open-code-primitives? #f) to keep many of the
primitive procedures from being compiled inline.

The FIXMEs in the C code cover the remaining stuff.


FEATURES

All the R4RS essential procedures are implemented, and some of the
remaining ones.  The numeric tower has only small exact integers and
double-length floats.  The macro appendix is unimplemented.

After an error occurs, you can continue the computation with 
(@proceed v) where v serves as the value of the offending expression.
Or there's a simple debugger and disassembler you can use -- 
enter (debug), then enter ? for help.  You can disassemble a
procedure from outside the debugger with, for example, (dis +).

It's possible to read and write fasl files like uts.fasl.
(@read-fasl-header input-port) checks the header and advances past it.
(@read-fasl input-port) returns the next object, or the eof object.
dis-fasl.scm has an example.

write-fasl.scm has corresponding output code.  For a machine startup
file, you should write only code objects for 0-argument procedures, in
the sequence they should be evaluated.  Get a code object with
(@closure->code (lambda () exp)).  compile-file.scm is an example.

(@system "ls") is like system("ls") is C.

I hope some people enjoy playing with this system even in its current
high-entropy state.  Bug reports, etc., are welcome.

-Darius
